<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01692</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</md:title>
    <md:uuid>79058f37-6a73-4b6a-909c-fcf2cbdaa80a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity represents the culmination of all of the factoring strategies learned in the unit. Polynomials are
  given with no context. Students must determine the type of polynomial, choose the correct strategy to implement, and
  then correctly factor each polynomial.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Allow students to work with a partner to answer questions 1 – 7 in the activity. This will encourage discussion
  and teamwork as they select the correct strategy and factor each polynomial.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->MLR 8: Discussion Supports:  Listening, Conversing</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content-->
    <para>As students are working in groups of two to choose a strategy to solve the polynomial problem, provide the following sentence frames for all to see: “We should do ____ next because . . .”, and “I noticed ___ , so I think . . . .” . " Encourage students to challenge each other when they disagree. This will help students clarify their reasoning when solving polynomial equations.  When students are discussing what is the same and different about their steps and solving strategies, assist students in using negatives and contractions correctly, such as do/don't, can/can't, is/isn't. </para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Design Principle(s):  Maximize meta-awareness</para>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->Action and Expression:  Appropriate Tools </para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content-->
    <para>Use this <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b8273b36fa39a8de034516b4563ecbd43298cbdd">Factor Pairs 1-100 </link>handout to help support students factoring polynomials of varying levels of difficulty. </para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Supports accessibility for Memory</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>Work with a partner on these problems. After each solution, discuss if there are multiple strategies that would work
  to factor the polynomial.</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(5y^3 − 15y^2 − 270y\) <para>You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
    <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
      <iframe>
        <video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
          <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/143008d05c5725e4e42c282ce15ca1124926fe73">
          <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dfa4acbaa03c6f1aa14f4e183fa95bbca397bb0b" srclang="en_us">
        </track></source></video>
      </iframe>
    </div>
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Factor out GCF of \(5y\). Then use the “undo FOIL” method on the polynomial of
    the form \(x^2 + bx +c\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    75m^3 + 12m\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Factor out GCF of \(3m\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(16x^2 + 24xy − 4x − 6y\)<emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Factor out GCF of \(2\). Then use the grouping method.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(2n^2 + 13n − 7\)<emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Use the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(121r^2 − s^2\)<emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Use the difference of squares method.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(25w^2 − 60w + 36\)<emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Use the perfect square trinomial method.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((3x + 1)^2 − 6(3x + 1) + 9\)<emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Use the substitution or perfect square trinomial method.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(16x^2 − 24xy + 9y^2 − 64\).<emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Use the grouping method on the first three terms. They are a perfect square trinomial. Then
    use the difference of squares on the result.</item>
</list>
<title> Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Pose the following question to students:</para>
<list>
  <item> If more than one strategy can be correctly implemented to factor a polynomial, is there an incorrect choice? (As
    long as you factor a polynomial down to its prime factors, you have correctly factored the polynomial. Sometimes
    more than one strategy will work.) </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Determining Factoring Method</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to determine which method of factoring will be best to apply.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe>
    <video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9381e45e777a0dff0ef45e7c94cae95307904b38">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/918aa697103004cbb2a58508615cee7f0875997f" srclang="en_us">
      https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9381e45e777a0dff0ef45e7c94cae95307904b38 </track></source></video>
  </iframe>
</div>
<title>6.7.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
  understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which strategy would you use to factor \(9x^2 − 24xy + 16y^2\)? What is the factored result?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>
  </caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> Answers </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> Feedback </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> trinomial square pattern; \((3x + 4y)^2\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The polynomial has the form \(a^2 − 2ab + b^2\). This
        fits the trinomial square pattern strategy. The signs do not match up with this answer since the second term is
        negative. The correct answer is \((3x − 4y)^2\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> grouping; \((3x − 4y)(3x + 4y)\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The polynomial has the form \(a^2 − 2ab + b^2\). This
        fits the trinomial square pattern strategy. Grouping is good when there are more than three terms. The correct
        answer is \((3x − 4y)^2\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> difference of squares; \((4x − 3y)^2\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The polynomial has the form \(a^2 − 2ab + b^2\). This
        fits the trinomial square pattern strategy. The correct answer is \((3x − 4y)^2\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> trinomial square pattern; \((3x − 4y)^2\) </entry>
      <entry> That’s correct! Check yourself: The polynomial has the form \(a^2 − 2ab + b^2\). Square the factors
        to check that they are equal to the original polynomial. </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>6.7.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
  their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
  to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></para>
<title>Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
<para>Let’s practice recognizing and implementing these strategies as we factor polynomials.</para>
<para>Remember, a polynomial is completely factored if, other than monomials, its factors are prime!</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 1</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(7x^3 − 21x^2 − 70x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor out the GCF.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - In the parentheses, is it a binomial, a trinomial, or are there more than three terms?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - “Undo” FOIL.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 2</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(4x^2 + 8bx − 4ax − 8ab\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - There are four terms. Use grouping.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. </para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 3</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(40x^2y + 44xy − 24y\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial with \(a \neq 1\) using the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. </para>
<title>Try It: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(27y^2 − 48\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(6x^2 − 12xc + 6bx − 12bc\). </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to factor these polynomials:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> difference of squares; \(3(3y − 4)(3y + 4)\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> grouping; \(6(x + b)(x − 2c)\) </item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
